    { "term": "Brazil", "matchText": "the enormous Portuguese colony", "type": "place", "definition": "The vast Portuguese colony on the eastern bulge of South America, and over the centuries of the trade the single largest destination for enslaved Africans anywhere — it imported close to five million people, more than ten times the number ever brought to the area that became the United States." },
    { "term": "Dutch West India Company", "matchText": "Dutch West India Company", "type": "group", "wikiSlug": "Dutch_West_India_Company" },
    { "term": "Chartered company", "matchText": "chartered company", "type": "concept", "definition": "A private corporation granted by its government a monopoly over trade in a region, along with the right to wage war, build forts, and govern — a business and a quasi-state at once. The Dutch West India Company and the Royal African Company were the great slaving examples." },
    { "term": "Barbados", "matchText": "Barbados", "type": "place", "wikiSlug": "Barbados" },
    { "term": "Sugar revolution", "matchText": "sugar revolution", "type": "concept", "definition": "The wholesale remaking of a Caribbean colony into a single sugar-and-slavery machine — land consolidated under a few great planters, enslaved Africans imported in bulk, and a death rate so high the population could only be maintained by constant new arrivals. Barbados was its first and purest case." },
